  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 101 PM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nStarting Thursday Night, a broad upper trough will move in from the \nwest, giving us increasing southwest flow aloft. Snow will spread \ninto the Continental Divide, accumulating across the peaks of the \nmountains through Friday and into early Saturday. Latest guidance \nshows the heaviest accumulations over the San Juans once again, with \nwidespread  2-4 inch amounts, though locally higher amounts to \naround 6 inches will be possible. Meanwhile, the rest of the \nContinental Divide is forecasted to get less than 2 inches, with \nlittle to no snow for the eastern mountains. Overall, the system is \nrelatively lacking in deep moisture, and QPF estimates have \ncontinued to decrease wit the last few sets of model runs, though \nthis could be offset somewhat by orographics.\n\nAs the system pushes east into the weekend, a cold front will push \nthrough Friday Night into Saturday, bringing colder temperatures to \nthe region as well as rain/snow showers. As of now, scattered \nshowers will move in behind the front over the Palmer Divide and \nPikes Peak area. High temperatures for the plains will drop from the \n50s-60s on Friday into the 40s to low-50s on Saturday, so even if El \nPaso County sees some flakes hit the ground, currently no \naccumulation is expected.\n\nWinds will also be an impact concern with this system, as a \nstrengthening upper jet maximum passes over us on the leading side \nof the trough. Most of the day on Friday, our CWA will be seeing \nstrong winds out of the southwest. Over the plains, latest NBM shows \ngusts 35-40 mph, though isolated gusts to 50 mph will be possible. \nThe San Luis Valley seems to be getting the strongest winds Friday \nafternoon, with gusts 50-55 mph expected. Probabilistic data only \nshows the potential to hit High Wind criteria in the valley for an \nhour or two Friday afternoon, with less than half the members, so \nwhile no highlights are needed as of right now the model trends will \nhave to be looked at for increases in gust potential.\n\nModels consistently show the system pushing east of us on Sunday, \nwith cool but mild weather returning through mid-late Monday. \nLooking further into next week, latest trends hint at another \nincoming trough bringing more snow to the mountains.",
